<div class="param-page">
  <div class="param-container">
    <a href="/parameters" class="btn btn-ghost" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem">← Back to Parameters Directory</a>

    <div class="param-card fu">
      <span class="badge badge-green">Chemical Parameter</span>
      <h1 style="font-size:2.2rem;font-weight:800;color:var(--white);margin-top:0.5rem">Ammonia & Ammonium</h1>
      
      <div class="param-meta">
        <div class="meta-box"><div class="meta-lbl">Chemical Formula</div><div class="meta-val">NH₃ / NH₄⁺</div></div>
        <div class="meta-box"><div class="meta-lbl">Safe Limit (WHO)</div><div class="meta-val">&lt; 0.5 mg/L</div></div>
        <div class="meta-box"><div class="meta-lbl">Category</div><div class="meta-val">Nutrient / Toxicant</div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="content-sec">
        <h2>Overview</h2>
        <p>Ammonia exists in water in two equilibrium forms: un-ionized ammonia (<span class="chem-formula">NH₃</span>) and ammonium ion (<span class="chem-formula">NH₄⁺</span>). Aquatic plants absorb ammonium into amino acids and proteins, but elevated concentrations promote harmful algal blooms and eutrophication.</p>
        
        <h2>Toxicity & Equilibrium</h2>
        <p>Un-ionized ammonia (<span class="chem-formula">NH₃</span>) is highly toxic to fish and aquatic fauna, damaging gill membranes and cellular processes. The equilibrium between toxic <span class="chem-formula">NH₃</span> and non-toxic <span class="chem-formula">NH₄⁺</span> shifts towards toxic <span class="chem-formula">NH₃</span> as pH and water temperature increase.</p>
        
        <h2>Nitrification & Oxygen Depletion</h2>
        <p>Nitrifying bacteria convert ammonium to nitrite (<span class="chem-formula">NO₂⁻</span>) and nitrate (<span class="chem-formula">NO₃⁻</span>). This biological process consumes significant amounts of dissolved oxygen (DO), stressing aquatic ecosystems.</p>
      </div>

      <div style="background:rgba(255,255,255,0.03);border:1px solid var(--border);border-radius:var(--r);padding:1.5rem;margin-top:2rem">
        <h3 style="color:var(--accent);font-size:1.1rem;margin-bottom:0.5rem">💡 Control & Treatment Methods</h3>
        <p style="font-size:0.9rem;color:var(--muted)">Biological nitrification towers, breakpoint chlorination, air stripping, and ion-exchange zeolites are primary engineering techniques used in municipal and industrial wastewater facilities to remove excess ammonia.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

